  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Quick turnaround to summerlike warmth to end \nSeptember and start October \n\nA burst of summerlike conditions are expected to peak later this \nweek as the calender flips from September to October. Fcst max \ntemps on Thu/Fri in the 75-85F range are +10-15 degrees above \nthe historical average and may be the warmest conditions \nobserved for the remainder of 2026. For context, the average \nlast date of max temp >=80F is 9/27 at State College, 9/29 at \nAltoona, 10/2 at Williamsport and 10/5 at Harrisburg. \n\n------------------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Showers expected Friday with cooler weekend \nforecast trending drier \n\nSome light rain could brush the northern tier later tonight into\nearly Thursday morning. The best odds for more widespread rain \nshowers are now focused on the late Thursday night (NW) through\nFriday time window as a cold front moves southeastward from the\neastern Great Lakes. Latest model data now in better agreement \nin suppressing rain chances farther to the south this weekend \nresulting in declining precip probs/increasing odds for a \nmainly dry first weekend of October. \n\nNoticeably cooler and less humid air will filter into the \nregion behind the front. 24hr maxT change on Saturday is fcst to\nbe 10-15 degrees lower vs. Friday across south central PA with \nhighs in the 60-70F range. The cooling trend continues through \nearly next week with no rain forecast through Wednesday. \n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 3: Increasing confidence in frost/freeze risk early\nnext week \n\nSecondary shot of seasonably cool/Autumn air is fcst to arrive\nearly next week in association with Canadian high pressure. The\noverall pattern and corresponding NBM temperatures are signaling\nan increased risk of frost/freeze conditions Monday and Tuesday\nnight. Some locations in the central Alleghenies may observe \nthe first frost/freeze of the season which is generally on par \nwith climo.",
